            <table class="size-table">
              <thead>
                <tr>
                  <th>US Kids Size</th>
                  <th>Footbed (CM)</th>
                  <th>Inches</th>
                  <th>EU Size</th>
                  <th>UK Size</th>
                  <th>Stage</th>
                </tr>
              </thead>
              <tbody>
                {kidsSizes.map(r => (
                  <tr>
                    <td class="primary-cell"><strong>US {r.us}</strong></td>
                    <td class="highlight-cell"><strong>{r.cm} cm</strong></td>
                    <td>{r.inch}″</td>
                    <td>EU {r.eu}</td>
                    <td>UK {r.uk}</td>
                    <td>{r.stage}</td>
                  </tr>
                ))}
              </tbody>
            </table>
          </div>
        </div>

      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- Editorial Guide -->
    <section class="editorial-band">
      <div class="page-inner prose-article">
        <h2 class="editorial-main-title">The Complete US to CM Shoe Sizing Guide: Metric Conversion, Math &amp; Measurement</h2>

        <p class="lead-paragraph">
          When buying footwear online from global retailers or choosing performance athletic sneakers, standard numerical sizes can be frustratingly inconsistent. Learning how to <strong>convert us shoe size to cm</strong> (centimeters) gives you an objective, standardized metric that bypasses regional sizing confusion. Centimeters represent the physical length of your footbed, providing an uncompromising baseline whether you are shopping American brands, Japanese imports, or European designer lines.
        </p>

        <h3>1. How Many CM Is a US Shoe Size? The Barleycorn to Metric Math</h3>
        <p>
          Shoe sizing in the United States is mathematically founded on the historic imperial barleycorn standard, where every full size step measures one-third of an inch (1/3″). In metric terms:
        </p>
        <ul>
          <li><strong>One Full Shoe Size Step:</strong> 1/3 inch = <strong>0.846 cm (8.46 mm)</strong></li>
          <li><strong>One Half Shoe Size Step:</strong> 1/6 inch = <strong>0.423 cm (4.23 mm)</strong></li>
        </ul>
        <p>
          Because of this linear scaling, as you move up in <strong>american shoe size cm</strong> gradations, footbed length expands by roughly 0.85 centimeters per full size and approximately 0.42 centimeters per half size. In common adult sizing:
        </p>
        <ul>
          <li>A Men's <strong>US 7</strong> fits a <strong>25.0 cm</strong> footbed.</li>
          <li>A Men's <strong>US 8</strong> fits a <strong>26.0 cm</strong> footbed.</li>
          <li>A Men's <strong>US 9</strong> fits a <strong>27.0 cm</strong> footbed.</li>
          <li>A Men's <strong>US 10</strong> fits a <strong>28.0 cm</strong> footbed.</li>
          <li>A Men's <strong>US 11</strong> fits a <strong>29.0 cm</strong> footbed.</li>
        </ul>

        <h3>2. Why Centimeters (CM) Are the Most Accurate Footwear Standard</h3>
        <p>
          Why do experienced runners, podiatrists, and footwear specialists advise checking <strong>shoe size in cm</strong> rather than relying solely on US number sizes?
        </p>
        <p>
          The primary reason is that US sizes are split by gender and influenced by brand-specific manufacturing lasts. A US Men's 8 does not equal a US Women's 8; an American women's size 8 is engineered for a 25.0 cm foot, whereas an American men's size 8 is engineered for a 26.0 cm foot.
        </p>
        <p>
          Conversely, <strong>centimeter to us shoe size</strong> conversion relies on an absolute physical unit. A foot measuring 26.5 cm is 26.5 cm worldwide. When you know your exact <strong>foot size by cm</strong>, you eliminate the risk of purchasing shoes that are too tight across the toes or excessively long at the heel.
        </p>

        <h3>3. Step-by-Step Guide: How to Measure Your Foot Size in CM at Home</h3>
        <p>
          To find your exact <strong>foot size to cm</strong> or determine <strong>feet size cm to us</strong>, follow this simple, podiatrist-approved paper tracing method:
        </p>
        <ol>
          <li><strong>Prepare Your Materials:</strong> Grab a blank sheet of paper larger than your foot, a hard ruler or measuring tape, and a fine-tip pen or pencil.</li>
          <li><strong>Position Your Heel:</strong> Place the sheet of paper on a hard, flat floor against a perpendicular wall. Step onto the paper with your heel lightly touching the wall.</li>
          <li><strong>Wear Your Usual Socks:</strong> Always measure wearing the thickness of socks you plan to use with that specific footwear type (e.g., thin dress socks for oxfords, padded athletic socks for runners).</li>
          <li><strong>Mark the Longest Toe:</strong> Standing straight with your weight balanced evenly across both feet, use the pen held completely vertical to draw a line at the outermost tip of your longest toe (which may be your big toe or second toe).</li>
          <li><strong>Measure the Distance:</strong> Step off the paper and measure the distance in centimeters from the wall-edge of the paper to your pencil mark. Measure to the nearest millimeter (e.g., 26.8 cm).</li>
          <li><strong>Repeat for Both Feet:</strong> Almost everyone has one foot slightly larger than the other. Always choose your footwear size based on the larger measurement.</li>
        </ol>

        <h3>4. Adding Toe Clearance: Insole Length vs. Foot Length</h3>
        <p>
          When reading <strong>footwear size in cm</strong> on brand tags or converting <strong>cm to us foot size</strong>, it is crucial to distinguish between <em>bare foot length</em> and <em>inner shoe cavity length</em>:
        </p>
        <p>
          On modern athletic footwear (such as Nike, Adidas, New Balance, and Asics), the CM measurement printed on the box tongue is your <strong>recommended foot length</strong>. The shoemaker has already built in approximately 10 to 15 mm of clearance beyond that figure to accommodate the natural forward splay of your foot during running and walking.
        </p>
        <p>
          If you are measuring a removable shoe insole directly with a tape measure, the insole itself will typically measure 1.0 to 1.5 cm longer than your actual foot. Do not subtract this clearance—it is vital for toe health and blister prevention.
        </p>

        <h3>5. Japanese (JP) Sizing and Mondopoint Equivalents</h3>
        <p>
          Japanese shoe sizing (JP) is the world's most transparent footwear standard because Japanese sizes are simply the foot length in centimeters. If a Japanese shoe is marked <strong>JP 27.0</strong>, it is engineered directly for a 27.0 cm foot (equal to US Men's 9.0).
        </p>
        <p>
          Similarly, the international military standard <strong>Mondopoint</strong> measures foot length and foot width in millimeters (e.g., 270/100 denotes 270 mm length and 100 mm width). Checking the JP or Mondopoint metric on imported ski boots, hiking boots, or Asian-market sneakers is the fastest way to confirm your true <strong>cm to american shoe size</strong> translation.
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- FAQ Section -->
    <section class="faq-band">
      <div class="page-inner">
        <h2 class="section-title">Frequently Asked Questions</h2>
        <p class="section-sub">Comprehensive answers to common questions about US shoe size to centimetre conversions.</p>

        <div class="faq-accordion-list">
          {faqs.map((f, i) => (
            <details class="faq-item" open={i === 0 ? true : undefined}>
